\section{Interlisp and the History of a Session}

In his \textit{History of Interlisp}, Warren Teitelman
describes the programming environment of BBN-LISP and
Interlisp as an aid to work already in progress. The
Break package let users examine a stopped computation.
History retained commands and responses so that a user
could replay or, for operations that recorded enough
information, undo some effects. DWIM (Do What I Mean)
could try context-sensitive spelling correction when the
evaluator encountered an undefined function or unbound
variable. This shortened some exploratory cycles but put
pressure on the interface to show users what correction
had actually been applied. Automated builds should not
silently rely on such a repair.

As Gabriel and Steele describe in \textit{The Evolution of
Lisp}, MASTERSCOPE built a database of relationships such
as which functions called other functions and where variables
were bound. An answer from that database is valid only for
the definitions analyzed; a subsequent edit must invalidate
or refresh the affected information. J Strother Moore's
\textit{Interlisp Virtual Machine Specification} defines
a separate boundary: abstract objects, Lisp operations,
bindings, I/O and interrupts on which the portable portion
of Interlisp could be built. An interactive environment
could thus move to another host without assuming that its
physical machine reproduced every implementation detail.

Moore specifies \texttt{SYSOUT} and \texttt{SYSIN} in terms
of saving and reconstructing virtual-machine state, explicitly
allowing exceptions for external resources such as open files.
Teitelman's File Package, by contrast, tracked which files owned
definitions and other named objects. It could rewrite files
containing changed definitions and flag changes with no file
to save them in. Keeping these operations separate is what
lets a developer choose between resuming an experiment and
publishing changes that colleagues can load into their own
sessions.

\section{Smalltalk and the Live Object World}

Fred Rivard's \textit{Smalltalk: A Reflective Language}
describes how classes, metaclasses, the compiler, and
execution contexts can be represented as objects. A browser
can inspect a class's methods, and a debugger can inspect
a reified context rather than cross a wholly separate
tooling interface. Defining a new implementation of a
selector changes future message lookup; it is a separate
question whether an active context migrates, restarts, or
finishes using its original compiled method. This resembles
the version problem in Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-154}, but rests
on Smalltalk message sends rather than CLOS generic dispatch.
Gilad Bracha and David Griswold's \textit{Strongtalk} shows
that adding a static checking discipline and using an
interactive Smalltalk environment are not mutually
exclusive; the harder question is what to require of
already existing code.

An image records a graph of objects that can be restored
as the starting state of an environment. Source text can
convey what classes and methods to install, but does not
necessarily record which objects the programmer created or
what state a workspace accumulated. A saved image can retain
that state, depending on the system's snapshot policy. By
contrast, shared source and declarative dependencies make
changes inspectable and builds repeatable. An image is
not a replacement for either. It also cannot generally
preserve operating-system resources by storing their
numeric identifiers: an open file or process handle belongs
to the original host session.

The resource boundary matters even without saving an image.
In \textit{Programming Languages as Operating Systems},
Matthew Flatt, Robert Bruce Findler, Shriram Krishnamurthi,
and Matthias Felleisen describe how MrEd's Scheme custodians
group threads and ports for explicit shutdown. An IDE and
its user's program may share a virtual machine without
sharing exactly the same lifetime. Dropping references to a
user program's values is not a substitute for terminating
its threads and closing its files.

\section{Worked Test: Restore Sharing, Recreate the Outside World}

Create two workspace objects that both refer to the same
mutable index. Save an image, start a new host process,
and restore it. After restoration, modify the index through
one workspace and verify that the other observes the
change. Equality of contents immediately after restore is
not enough; serialization must preserve aliasing as well
as values. Now exercise an associated file-backed record.
The host file must be reopened or reported unavailable
according to an explicit restore policy; a saved integer
must never be accepted simply because it happens to be
a usable descriptor in the new process.

Finally export the changed definitions and reconstruct
them from a clean base without the saved image. Run the
application-level tests in both configurations. If only
the restored image works, compare logged edits and
declared inputs for omitted definitions or state. This
complements Chapter~44 of \textit{The Modern Compiler
Dossier}: a checkpoint offers continuity, whereas a
hermetic compilation capsule offers replay from declared
inputs. Both are useful, but they promise different things.
